  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nUpper analysis has the middle Mississippi Valley positioned under \nnorthwest flow with strong upper ridging over the southwest US and a \nclosed low impacting the northeast US. Surface high pressure over \ncentral IL early this morning has kept skies mostly clear and winds \nlight. Temperatures as of 2 am have dropped into the upper 50s to \nlow 60s with dewpoint depressions ranging from 0 to a couple \ndegrees. Because of this, patchy low lying fog will be possible \nthrough about sunrise. Otherwise, seasonal and mostly dry weather is \nexpected through Tuesday with high temperatures today and tomorrow \nexpected to be in the upper 70s to middle 80s.\n\nBy Wednesday, a mid-level shortwave will track through the southern \nCanadian provinces and into the northern Great Lakes Region, sending \na cold front through Illinois. This will bring our next chance for \nshowers and storms. Out ahead of the front, temperatures will climb \nwell into the 80s with steady moisture return bringing dewpoints \nback into the upper 60s to low 70s. Moderately strong instability is \nexpected to develop by Wednesday afternoon with seasonally strong \nshear around 30 kts nearest to the front. Forecast soundings depict \ninverted-V T/Td profiles, implying evaporative cooling could rapidly \nsend parcels toward the surface and locally increase winds. A \ncapping inversion will be in place most of the day, but will erode \nby late afternoon/early evening as the front approaches and lapse \nrates steepen. Although the better forcing will be displaced to our \nnorth, a few strong to marginally severe storms could develop late \nWednesday afternoon and evening, posing a threat for damaging wind \ngusts and isolated large hail.\n\nNorthwest flow remains largely in place through late this week, \ntransitioning to zonal by the weekend. A few weak shortwaves \nrippling through the upper flow may spark occasional spotty showers \nthrough the end of the week, though most will stay dry. Longer range \nguidance shows a more defined wave/front moving through the Midwest \nstates sometime between Saturday night and Sunday, which could bring \nanother chance for showers and storms. Temperatures will remain \nseasonable through the end of the week, then turn warmer later this \nweekend into early next week with the NBM showing upper 80s to near \n90 degree highs.",
